# What must never be committed, and why. Four of these lines are load-bearing
# for something else in the tree; the rest are ordinary Python noise.

__pycache__/
*.pyc
.venv/
.env

# The hashed audience export. engine/audience.py writes it from a list that
# lives in outreach-engine; the raw list never leaves the machine that built it.
queue/*.csv

# Scratch output. Nothing downstream reads it.
out/

# The offline walk's artifacts. `python tools/walk.py` rebuilds every file in
# here from fixtures in one second, so a committed copy would only ever be a
# stale second opinion about what the engine does. The marker file inside is
# what stops the tool deleting a directory it did not write.
demo/

# Somebody else's creative. An operator opens an ad's snapshot URL and saves
# the file here by hand so engine/analyse.py can attach it to one model call -
# nothing in this repository fetches one. `research/media/*` covers EVERY
# suffix, not just the video ones below, which is what makes `git add
# research/media` safe to have never been written. The README is the one file
# in there that is ours.
research/media/*
!research/media/README.md

# Renders. reel-engine shoots the video and build.yml keeps it as an Actions
# artifact for 14 days; an MP4 has no business in git history.
*.mp4
*.mov
*.m4v
*.webm

# NOT IGNORED, deliberately, and each one has a reader:
#   queue/**/*.jpg          the still reel-engine shot, committed beside the
#                           job by build.yml so the approval issue can show it.
#                           Add a *.jpg rule here and the second tap loses its
#                           picture.
#   research/corpus/*.json  the evidence. One file per analysed ad; git history
#                           is the audit log.
#   research/patterns.json  what the corpus counts to.
#   research/selection.json the weekly report, written on every live run.
#   research/measurements.json  what our own launched ads did.
